@pgxsinkit/pgwasm-pg-dump
=========================

This package republishes compiled third-party software and includes code that began as
a copy of third-party code. These attributions are permanent.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ElectricSQL — @electric-sql/pglite-tools (the pg_dump wrapper under `src/`)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The code that runs pg_dump over a database's wire (`src/pg-dump.ts`,
`src/emscripten.ts`, `src/wasm.ts`) began as a copy of ElectricSQL's PGlite
(`packages/pglite-tools`, and the artefact loading of `packages/pglite-utils`):

  Source:  https://github.com/electric-sql/pglite
  License: PostgreSQL License (PGlite is dual-licensed Apache-2.0 / PostgreSQL
           License, at the licensee's choice; pgxsinkit takes it under the
           PostgreSQL License)
  Copyright (c) ElectricSQL and contributors

Owned outright (ADR-0062); compatibility with PGlite is an explicit anti-goal. The
PostgreSQL License text is in LICENSE.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The artefacts, and what is compiled into them
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

`artefacts/` holds two files: assets of the pgwasm-postgres release 18.6.0
(https://github.com/pgxsinkit/pgwasm-postgres/releases/tag/18.6.0), the release that also holds `@pgxsinkit/pgwasm-c`'s server, republished
byte-identical (each file's size and sha256 are pinned in `src/artefact-pins.ts`, from
the release's manifest.json checked against its SHA256SUMS):

  - `pg_dump.wasm`: pg_dump from PostgreSQL 18.6 (it reports
    `pg_dump (PostgreSQL) 18.6`), compiled to WebAssembly;
  - `pg_dump.js`, its Emscripten-generated loader.

pgxsinkit/pgwasm-postgres (https://github.com/pgxsinkit/pgwasm-postgres) builds them
from PostgreSQL 18.6 (the upstream tag REL_18_6, commit
724edf9bde9d356724ad384a2e196edc3c9f80f7) plus a patch series and an overlay derived
from ElectricSQL's PGlite fork of PostgreSQL
(https://github.com/electric-sql/postgres-pglite at
b133782cd759f08b3aeb263b80a963b39c7b7af1; PostgreSQL License, Copyright (c)
ElectricSQL and contributors). Release 18.6.0 is pgwasm-postgres commit
efcbf6eee6dacc454fcb123e3730ad8f051a6eda, built inside the builder image
ghcr.io/pgxsinkit/pgwasm-builder@sha256:c9eacc51a7c25d67aef3daa418a39592a97f471aff099fa5a88fa1c82107435d
(whose recipe is that repository's `builder/Containerfile`); its manifest.json records
every file's size and sha256, and the release was published only because a rebuild of
the tag gave the gated build's manifest. pg_dump is built with the server, in the same run. Both files are
byte-identical to the pg_dump ElectricSQL published in `@electric-sql/pglite-tools`
0.4.8.

What is compiled in comes from the build (`build-pglite.sh`,
`src/bin/pg_dump/Makefile`) and from the binary itself:

  - pg_dump is linked from its own objects, libpq and PostgreSQL's frontend
    libraries (fe_utils, pgcommon, pgport), with ElectricSQL's `pglitec.c`, the host
    shims that route libpq's socket calls to the loader's callbacks;
  - the configure line (--with-zlib --with-libxml --with-icu --with-openssl=no
    --without-llvm) puts -lz and -lxml2 on every program's link line. zlib is
    compiled in: its inflate messages are in pg_dump.wasm. No code from libxml2 or
    ICU is: none of their messages or names are in the binary (pg_dump calls none of
    them, and the link keeps only what is called), so their notices are not
    reproduced here (`@pgxsinkit/pgwasm-c`'s NOTICE has them). The binary is the
    same byte for byte as ElectricSQL's, whose configure line also had
    --with-libxslt: no libxslt code was in it either;
  - Emscripten 3.1.74 (the builder image's) provides the loader's runtime and the
    system libraries: musl libc and LLVM's compiler-rt. pg_dump is C, and no C++
    runtime (libc++, libc++abi) is in the binary.

Not included: OpenSSL (--with-openssl=no), GSSAPI, LZ4 and Zstandard (not
configured), and the backend's own third-party code (its regular-expression engine,
Snowball), which pg_dump does not contain.

Each component's notice follows, copied from the file named with it.

